~ start
NEWS ANNOUNCER: [speed=1.0][b]BREAKING NEWS![/b] TANAW, the Tropical Atmospheric Navigation and Advanced Weather Bureau, is tracking a severe weather disturbance expected to make landfall on November 8. Although the storm is still offshore, local authorities in Kanlungan City have suspended classes at all levels starting tomorrow to give families time to prepare.[/speed]

NEWS ANNOUNCER: [speed=1.0]Residents in low-lying areas, especially agricultural communities, are advised to secure their homes and prepare for heavy rainfall and possible flash floods. Please stay tuned for further updates and evacuation advisories.[/speed]

DODONG: [speed=1.2][wave amp=2 freq=3]Sweet! No classes tomorrow! I can play my Game Boy all day![/wave][/speed]

NONI: [speed=1.3][wave amp=3 freq=4]No school?! Yay! Kuya, let's play outside in the rain tomorrow! We can bring Kiki, too![/wave][/speed]

VALERIE: [speed=1.1]Are you two serious?! Didn't you hear the news? It's a typhoon, not just some ordinary rain![/speed]

NONI: [speed=1.0]But it's just rain...[/speed]

VALERIE: [speed=1.0]Not when it's a big storm, Noni![/speed]

PILI: ...

PILI: [speed=0.9]Ernesto... did you hear that? They said the low-lying areas could flood.[/speed]

PILI: [speed=0.9]That's us, isn't it? We're right here in the valley.[/speed]

ERNESTO: [speed=0.9]Pili, we've lived here our whole lives. Our parents lived here, too. We've had storms before and we've always been fine.[/speed]

PILI: [speed=0.9]But what if this one's different?[/speed]

ERNESTO: [speed=1.0]The news always makes these things sound worse than they are. Look at our coconut trees. They've stood through every storm we've had.[/speed]

PILI: [speed=0.9]But the river-[/speed]

ERNESTO: [speed=1.1]And what about the harvest? The Millgate trucks are coming Tuesday morning. If the copra gets wet before then, all those months of work could go to waste![/speed]

PILI: [speed=0.9]I'm worried about the harvest, too, Ernesto. I am. But what if the river overflows? We have children to think about.[/speed]

ERNESTO: [speed=1.2][shake rate=3 level=2]I know that! I'm doing all this for them![/shake][/speed]

ERNESTO: ...

ERNESTO: [speed=0.8]This deal could finally give us some breathing room. We could pay our debts, buy what the children need... and give Ollie the birthday he deserves.[/speed]

ERNESTO: [speed=0.9]I can't just let all that work go to waste because of a storm that's still out at sea.[/speed]

PILI: [speed=0.9]But Ernesto, shouldn't we at least check-[/speed]

ERNESTO: [speed=1.0]We've lived here for generations, Pili. Our trees have stood through every storm. There's no reason to panic now.[/speed]

PILI: [speed=0.7]...I just don't want anything to happen to the children.[/speed]

ERNESTO: [speed=0.9]Nothing's going to happen. We'll be fine.[/speed]

PILI: [speed=0.6]...I hope you're right.[/speed]

ERNESTO: [speed=0.9]I'll check the drying roofs later. We can't afford to lose the copra now, not with the trucks coming. That's all we need to worry about tonight.[/speed]

KIKI: [speed=1.0]Nay?[/speed]

PILI: [speed=1.0]Hmm? What is it, anak?[/speed]

KIKI: [speed=1.2][wave amp=2 freq=3]Up![/wave][/speed]

PILI: [speed=1.0]Oh, you want me to carry you? Come here, sweetheart.[/speed]

ERNESTO: [speed=0.9]Dodong, put that gameboy away. It's getting late.[/speed]

DODONG: [speed=1.1]Aw, Tay! Just one more level![/speed]

ERNESTO: [speed=1.0]I said put it away. You can play tomorrow.[/speed]

DODONG: [speed=0.9]Fine...[/speed]

[speed=0.7]Nanay said our coconut trees keep us safe.[/speed]

[speed=0.6]But... why did Nanay look so scared?[/speed]

[speed=0.8]Tatay said we'd be fine.[/speed]

[speed=0.6]The trees will protect us, right?[/speed]

[speed=0.5]I'm still going to have my birthday party... right?[/speed]

=> END